    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Sorry for not replying sooner. This is one idea:<div><a href=\"https://gist.github.com/jcheng5/cedbbdde1d965fb332a5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://gist.github.com/jcheng5/cedbbdde1d965fb332a5&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792394157095000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Tm8NQkAULuK-CHSyLila5\">https://gist.github.com/jcheng5/cedbbdde1d965fb332a5</a><br></div><div><br></div><div>Basically your observeEvent will help build up a list of sliderUI, and callModule(slider). And the renderUI will just display the list.</div><div><br></div><div>I just realized, I think you&#39;re going to find this slightly problematic at some point because adding a slider will reset all the other sliders to default values. You can fix this by changing the list of sliderUI to a list of slider id&#39;s. Then the renderUI should be modified to take the list of slider id&#39;s, and return a sliderUI with the default value set to input$duplicateSlider<i>X</i> if that value exists.</div><div><br></div><div>Sorry, this is much more difficult than it needs to be. We&#39;re talking about adding an &quot;appendUI&quot; type of function to Shiny itself.</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Mon, Mar 28, 2016 at 7:19 AM MySchizo Buddy &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">myschi...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
